# 1. What we are building

Athera is an adaptive workspace for owner-operated and multi-location businesses. It starts as a minimal, generic workspace and reshapes itself around how the business actually works, based on observed behavior. The owner always stays in control.

MVP scope: a flexible workspace (work items, custom fields, generated views), an adaptation loop (detect, suggest, preview, accept/dismiss, undo), and instrumentation that proves adaptations are accepted and still used two weeks later.

Out of scope for now: voice, Ollama, file scanning, Gmail/calendar integrations, teams and permissions, billing, native apps. Do not modify or import legacy voice or Ollama code. All new work lives in the paths listed in section 4.

# 2. Non-negotiable principles

1. Owner control. Nothing changes the workspace without an explicit Accept. No silent adaptations, ever.
2. Reversibility. Every accepted change can be undone in one click, and undo restores data exactly as it was.
3. Generic core. work_items has only title, due_date, notes. Everything else is a custom field. Never add domain columns such as client, status, or amount to work_items.
4. Data-driven UI. No component may hard-code a custom field or a view. Components render from field_definitions and view_definitions.
5. Explainable. Every suggestion carries a plain-language reason that cites concrete evidence from the user's own data.
6. Deterministic detection. Detectors are pure functions with no network calls and no LLM calls. An LLM may later be used only to phrase the reason text, never to decide whether to suggest.
7. Measurable. Every user-visible adaptation event is logged to the events table.
8. Quiet by default. At most one pending suggestion is shown at a time. Dismissed suggestions are never re-shown for 30 days.

# 3. Stack and conventions

- Next.js (App Router), TypeScript in strict mode, Tailwind CSS, Supabase (Auth + Postgres). Use @supabase/ssr for server and browser clients.
- Validation with zod at every server boundary (server actions, route handlers). Infer types from schemas where possible.
- Tests with Vitest. Pure logic (detectors, engine, operations, templates) must have unit tests. Write the tests first for detectors and operations.
- No `any`. No non-null assertions without a comment. No default exports except Next.js pages/layouts/route files.
- Server code never trusts the client for workspace_id. Derive it from the authenticated user via getCurrentWorkspace().
- All writes go through server actions or route handlers. No direct client-side table writes.
- Dates: store as UTC, date-only fields as `date`. Use date-fns for date math, never manual millisecond arithmetic.
- Naming: files kebab-case, components PascalCase, DB columns snake_case, TS properties camelCase (map at the data-access layer).
- Prefer small, composable modules. Max ~200 lines per file; split when larger.
- Before adding a dependency, check whether the repo already has one that does the job. State why in your summary when you add one.

